  <h2>The Scaling Crisis</h2>

  <p>TechFlow's problems were typical of successful startups hitting their first major growth inflection point. Their founders had developed an effective, relationship-based sales approach that worked beautifully at small scale but created multiple bottlenecks as they tried to grow.</p>

  <div class="challenge-box">
    <div class="challenge-title">🚨 The Core Challenges</div>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Knowledge Transfer Crisis:</strong> Founders' institutional knowledge wasn't transferring to new hires</li>
      <li><strong>Inconsistent Messaging:</strong> Each rep developed their own approach, leading to wildly different results</li>
      <li><strong>Research Bottleneck:</strong> Manual prospect research was consuming 60% of selling time</li>
      <li><strong>Quality Control Issues:</strong> No systematic way to ensure call quality across the growing team</li>
      <li><strong>Coaching Limitations:</strong> Sales managers couldn't provide real-time guidance to multiple reps</li>
      <li><strong>Performance Variance:</strong> Top performers were 8x more productive than bottom performers</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="quote-box">
    <div class="quote-text">"We were successful despite our process, not because of it. Our founders were great at building relationships and closing deals, but when we tried to hire more people to do the same thing, everything fell apart. We knew we needed to systematize our approach without losing the personal touch that made us successful."</div>
    <div class="quote-author">— Sarah Chen, VP of Sales, TechFlow Solutions</div>
  </div>

  <h2>The Before Picture: Manual Processes at Breaking Point</h2>

  <div class="results-comparison">
